  => the baseline FAILS C1 at every shift, by the full size of the change it
     makes and does not report. C1 is a real instrument, not a formality.

NON-VACUITY -- a WRONG ledger must also fail C1

  shift    actual change      correct rpt     N2 sign flip N3 upstream only    C1 ok       N2       N3
      4 -3.985598499e-06 -3.985598499e-06  3.985598499e-06  0.000000000e+00     pass     FAIL     FAIL
     -4  3.999891249e-06  3.999891249e-06 -3.999891249e-06  3.999891249e-06     pass     FAIL     pass

  => the correct ledger passes; N2 fails at both signs; N3 passes for the sign it
     happens to cover and FAILS for the other. So C1 discriminates a right ledger
     from two plausible wrong ones, and N3 shows why both fill paths must be
     exercised -- a one-sided test would have reported success.
